        "Stem": "Underapplied overhead occurs when:",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "Underapplied manufacturing overhead occurs when the overhead applied to production (using the predetermined overhead rate) is less than the actual overhead incurred during the period. The formula is Applied MOH = POHR x Actual Activity Base. If Applied MOH < Actual MOH, the difference is underapplied overhead and must be closed to Cost of Goods Sold or prorated among Work-in-Process, Finished Goods, and COGS at period-end. Underapplied overhead means product costs were understated during the period. In contrast, overapplied overhead (Applied MOH > Actual MOH) means too much overhead was allocated to products. This concept is tested under CMA Part 1 Section D.2 (Cost Management — overhead allocation). A common exam trap is confusing overapplied and underapplied: candidate should memorize that 'under' means applied falls short of actual, while 'over' means applied exceeds actual.",
