        "Stem": "At Harbor Financial Group, operational managers are responsible for implementing and maintaining effective internal controls within their departments. A separate risk management function develops policies and monitors compliance. Internal audit provides independent assurance to the board. Under the Three Lines of Defense model, which line does the risk management function represent?",
        "CorrectChoice": "C",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "The Three Lines of Defense model, developed by the Institute of Internal Auditors (IIA), provides a framework for clarifying roles and responsibilities in governance and risk management. The first line of defense consists of operational management — the people who own and manage risk on a day-to-day basis by implementing and maintaining internal controls within their business units. The second line of defense consists of risk management and compliance functions that oversee risk — they develop policies, frameworks, and monitoring mechanisms to support the first line in managing risk effectively. They provide guidance, challenge, and oversight but are not independent of management. The third line of defense is internal audit, which provides independent, objective assurance to the board and senior management on the effectiveness of governance, risk management, and internal control. In this scenario, the risk management function that develops policies and monitors compliance is the second line — it provides oversight and support to the first line (operational managers) while itself being subject to independent assurance from the third line (internal audit).",
