        "Stem": "Eastgate Hospital is evaluating its data security risks. The board considers the following options: (1) discontinuing the online patient portal and reverting to paper records, (2) purchasing cybersecurity insurance with a $50,000 deductible, (3) implementing multi-factor authentication and encryption for all patient data, and (4) accepting that no system is perfectly secure and budgeting for potential breach notification costs. Under the standard risk response framework, these options represent, respectively:",
        "CorrectChoice": "A",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "The four standard risk responses under the COSO ERM framework (which also apply to internal control risk assessment) are: (1) Risk avoidance — eliminating the activity that gives rise to the risk. Discontinuing the online patient portal entirely avoids the data security risk by removing the source of the risk, though it also eliminates the benefits of the portal. (2) Risk sharing/transfer — shifting some portion of the risk to another party, typically through insurance, outsourcing, or hedging. Purchasing cybersecurity insurance transfers the financial impact of a breach to the insurer in exchange for premiums and the deductible. (3) Risk reduction/mitigation — implementing controls that reduce either the likelihood or the impact of the risk. Multi-factor authentication and encryption reduce the likelihood of unauthorized access and the impact if a breach occurs. (4) Risk acceptance — acknowledging that residual risk remains after other responses are applied, and budgeting for the consequences. Accepting the reality of imperfect security and budgeting for breach notification costs is risk acceptance. The distinction between these four responses is tested frequently on the CMA exam — candidates must be able to classify a described action into the correct response category.",
