        "ProductionStatus": "Production",
        "Stem": "A financial services company requires that: (1) each employee is granted access only to the specific systems and data needed for their job function, (2) access rights are reviewed quarterly by department managers, and (3) when an employee transfers to a different department, their prior access rights are automatically revoked before new rights are granted. These three controls collectively implement which fundamental information security principles?",
        "CorrectChoice": "D",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "These three controls collectively implement the core principles of logical access control: (1) Least privilege — granting each employee access only to the specific systems and data needed for their job function. This minimizes the potential damage from errors, misuse, or compromised credentials by limiting what each user can access. (2) Periodic access review — quarterly review of access rights by department managers ensures that access remains appropriate as job responsibilities change. Access rights tend to accumulate over time (\"privilege creep\") as employees change roles, take on temporary projects, or cover for absent colleagues — periodic review is essential to detect and remove unnecessary access. (3) Segregation of duties in access administration — the \"clean sweep\" approach of revoking old access before granting new access during a transfer prevents the accumulation of incompatible access rights across roles. This is a specific application of segregation of duties at the IT access level. Together, these three controls address the fundamental objectives of logical access security: users should have only the access they need (least privilege), that access should be regularly validated (review), and role transitions should not create back doors or accumulated privileges (clean transfer).",
