        "ProductionStatus": "Production",
        "Stem": "Bayside Bank is selecting a cloud service provider to host its customer loan processing system. Before signing the contract, the bank's risk management team reviews the provider's SOC 2 Type II report, requires evidence of the provider's own disaster recovery testing, and negotiates a right-to-audit clause in the service agreement. Under the risk assessment component of COSO, these activities are best described as:",
        "CorrectChoice": "B",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "Vendor (third-party) risk assessment is an integral part of the COSO risk assessment component, particularly when an entity outsources critical business functions or relies on external service providers. The activities described represent due diligence and ongoing risk management of third-party relationships: (1) Reviewing the SOC 2 Type II report — a SOC 2 Type II report is an independent auditor's examination of a service organization's controls over a period of time (typically 6-12 months), providing evidence that the provider's controls are suitably designed and operating effectively. This is a standard tool for assessing third-party control environments. (2) Requiring evidence of disaster recovery testing — this assesses the provider's operational resilience and ability to maintain service continuity, directly addressing the risk that the bank's operations could be disrupted by a failure at the provider. (3) Negotiating a right-to-audit clause — this preserves the bank's ability to independently verify the provider's controls on an ongoing basis, rather than relying solely on the provider's representations or third-party reports. Under COSO, entity-level risk assessment must extend to outsourced service providers because the entity retains ultimate responsibility for internal control even when operations are performed by third parties.",
