        "ExplanationCorrect": "The COSO Internal Control—Integrated Framework (2013) identifies three categories of objectives that internal control helps an entity achieve: (1) operations objectives — relating to effectiveness and efficiency of the entity's operations, including operational and financial performance goals and safeguarding assets against loss; (2) reporting objectives — relating to internal and external financial and non-financial reporting, encompassing reliability, timeliness, transparency, and other terms established by regulators, standard setters, or the entity's policies; and (3) compliance objectives — relating to adherence to laws and regulations to which the entity is subject. These three categories are distinct but overlapping — a single control can serve more than one objective category. The CMA exam frequently tests this fundamental COSO concept as it establishes the scope and purpose of all subsequent internal control discussion. Note that safeguarding of assets falls under operations objectives in the 2013 Framework, which was a key update from the 1992 version where it was a separate category.",
