        "ExplanationCorrect": "This multi-tiered approval structure is an example of authorization controls, which are a fundamental category of preventive controls within the safeguarding controls domain. Authorization controls ensure that transactions are executed only in accordance with management's general or specific approval. Two types exist: (1) general authorization — management establishes policies and guidelines (e.g., 'department heads may approve routine purchases up to $5,000 within budget'), and the organization follows these policies without case-by-case approval; and (2) specific authorization — management approves individual transactions on a case-by-case basis (e.g., the CFO and VP must each sign off on purchases over $50,000). Meridian's policy uses both types: general authorization for routine purchases under $5,000 and specific authorization for purchases above that threshold, with escalating approval requirements for larger amounts. This tiered approach is a standard internal control practice that balances operational efficiency with financial oversight.",
