        "ProductionStatus": "Draft",
        "Stem": "Apex Manufacturing discovered through a monthly inventory reconciliation that 200 units of raw material valued at $15,000 were recorded in the perpetual inventory system but were not physically present in the warehouse. Management immediately: (1) adjusted the inventory records to reflect the physical count, (2) investigated the discrepancy and determined that a receiving clerk had been entering fictitious receipts, and (3) implemented a requirement that all receiving reports be matched to purchase orders before inventory records are updated. Which of these actions is best classified as a corrective control?",
        "CorrectChoice": "B",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "Corrective controls are actions taken to remedy a problem after it has been detected. Under COSO Principle 12, the full control lifecycle involves: preventive controls (stop problems before they occur), detective controls (identify problems after they occur), and corrective controls (fix the problems and address the root cause to prevent recurrence). In this scenario, action (3) — implementing a purchase-order-to-receiving-report matching requirement — is a corrective control because it addresses the root cause of the fictitious receipt problem by adding a preventive check. Action (1), the inventory adjustment, is a correction (fixing the specific error) but not a 'corrective control' in the formal internal control sense — the distinction is between a one-time fix and a structural control change. Action (2) is investigation (part of detection/follow-up). The CMA exam distinguishes between: (a) correcting an error (making the adjustment) and (b) implementing a corrective control (changing the system to prevent recurrence). The latter — addressing the root cause through structural change — is the proper classification.",
