        "ProductionStatus": "Draft",
        "Stem": "The IIA's International Standards for the Professional Practice of Internal Auditing require that the chief audit executive (CAE) report to a level within the organization that allows the internal audit activity to fulfill its responsibilities. Which reporting relationship best supports internal audit independence and objectivity under IIA Standard 1110?",
        "CorrectChoice": "D",
        "ExplanationCorrect": "IIA Standard 1110 (Organizational Independence) requires that the chief audit executive report to a level within the organization that allows the internal audit activity to fulfill its responsibilities. The CAE must confirm organizational independence to the board at least annually. IIA Standard 1110.A1 further specifies that the internal audit activity must be free from interference in determining the scope of internal auditing, performing work, and communicating results. The most effective reporting structure for supporting independence is dual reporting: functional reporting to the board (specifically the audit committee) for strategic direction, authority, and independence, and administrative reporting to the CEO for day-to-day operations, budgeting, and human resources. IIA Standard 1130 (Impairment to Independence or Objectivity) addresses situations where independence or objectivity is impaired in fact or appearance — if the CAE reports to the CFO (Choice A), independence is potentially impaired because internal audit is reviewing financial reporting processes that the CFO is responsible for. The CMA exam tests the principle that internal audit should be positioned high enough in the organization to be objective about the functions it audits.",
        "SourceDescription": "Original CMA Part 1 exam-style practice item. Not real CMA exam content and not copied from official samples. Replaces prior clone rotation group for internal audit organizational placement and reporting structure coverage.",
        "QuestionID": "P1-E-R33",
